#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "977a9bdb-586d-525e-97f5-161f4c856802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089233} {:answer/id #uuid "a9154876-3666-554c-9b15-edefad852019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089234} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0efa11-25a1-5663-bab7-ef7b8b9c2a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089235} {:answer/id #uuid "bcbc0eaf-cb1a-50be-8716-02ce17955d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089236} {:answer/id #uuid "3362bcf0-4c15-5689-8038-b853f3729683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae0efa11-25a1-5663-bab7-ef7b8b9c2a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089235} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fc90b6e-ce90-55be-b99a-c6a56d4dcecd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089232}] :question/id #uuid "affd1161-3d1a-5157-8f5d-ad61f32ab379" :question/math-academy-id "q-12126" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-3), (-3, 2), (2, 4)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $R_{-90^{∘}}$ by an angle of $90^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n1. $(-3, 2)$\n2. $(4,-2)$\n3. $(-4, 2)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9fcb36c-7a96-513a-82f8-54b97a4bf1ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089239} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bb609c-de45-50c9-9616-e97c935254c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089240} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b84b93-3c8e-5f3c-a3d7-c8064cac79a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089241} {:answer/id #uuid "f249a068-6568-50d9-8d1c-ef2ec4bcc3e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089242} {:answer/id #uuid "32f445f6-ae0d-5f95-b4ea-d87325dd29d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1b84b93-3c8e-5f3c-a3d7-c8064cac79a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089241} :answer-field/id #uuid "b121d8ac-2514-5fe4-a61c-d75bd3afa8a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089238}] :question/id #uuid "4ff1deaa-4798-5008-88bc-6c10716485ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-12130" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-1), (1, 4), (1,-1)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $R_{π/2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{π}{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n1. $(4,-2)$\n2. $(1,-2)$\n3. $(1, 1)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9af7361f-b172-571c-9a0c-eb8a5cf0e944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286118} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9f4a6f-c98a-5b8b-b475-dbb8ce8b7ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286119} {:answer/id #uuid "4771e21e-cd38-5610-8e3e-29db3d43a63e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286120} {:answer/id #uuid "3a35e855-f108-53bc-8651-fb1e28f858b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286121} {:answer/id #uuid "5034ad09-915d-54bc-a17c-b4c71cbdf70f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e9f4a6f-c98a-5b8b-b475-dbb8ce8b7ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286119} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf293de0-0223-54aa-bbaa-23e0de8be281" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ca0ac68-14b1-589e-965f-d2a01a29fa17" :question/math-academy-id "q-160647" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(5,-1),(2,0),(-2,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-1,-5)$\n- $(0,2)$\n- $(-1,-2)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-5),(0,-2),(-1,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afa58e3f-1404-5bee-89f4-249fd02760ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/39168e3de9d42254e0c1528ab7ed40f2a4c0e88e9570705b773e3875d5057df2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283727} {:answer/id #uuid "f8aad999-521b-5aaf-89a4-73a2f1cffc33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/b66fd6bb3e9b37fd3a72927a1fb4df87eaffea3bf5579e681fee3aeda4885e75.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283728} {:answer/id #uuid "6516a4d4-b0e5-52e6-a17d-f93ce9905da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/aa78e1ab38afbc0cd7bf92061d176a816c7a8a0e1d08c8825486c45dbe8fb5f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283729} {:answer/id #uuid "067294cd-2914-54e2-abbc-ee5e06656f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/c95c1f93ccc6c6c2ff7264ac5bca90680ff2c3c745d95acb0f81d288bf4e498a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283730} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1c7c10-9d73-56d1-99b2-51f5c9bb8ebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/d64f49a072fd7841d1d1b81e396673f3addcfa05005128e969dbb6ea4697d358.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afa58e3f-1404-5bee-89f4-249fd02760ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/39168e3de9d42254e0c1528ab7ed40f2a4c0e88e9570705b773e3875d5057df2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283727} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf981b3-4d4d-5958-b48b-c58b6b3c7ded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c0ad4a3-22f9-57a4-8323-31593f95cf81" :question/math-academy-id "q-81823" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/0c2f0240b962701c2eb2ee34a0eddc164afb8e1a677a5ed7f81fe65efb890358.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/578b3315028bf5a19a4b6e17d228db08b41f24dee63e5cb7f8d601ce667785f4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bae14332-1587-5084-970b-bde383f5e7ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/cf449d44237c85963bf4fbb134da290b39aac4ebd0b128313052cbd1970db67f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286132} {:answer/id #uuid "95dc36df-d626-56ea-bf9e-b872b342502b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/5b4684361e541532d1be24de046f17201811146fbb0413222c5100e5a3eb80dc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286133} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8e74b7-f6cd-5b7a-a08e-85bc2aeca52e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/979157d993a86617fabf4b2dfacc6d4c1d973885b42be081b4bcffb36058e091.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286134} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ab87b0-1024-5d8e-8ccd-4b4c43d6488d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/4bf42e3bdeb61feccd68cf4eeb9e3c4a40e8b903a5739df77c993e9aa4abc993.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286135} {:answer/id #uuid "adcd67c9-2bae-5a2f-88ce-cd3143fc6cad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/f4d4d52cb24bd1e3dd4bc37736533693ff89e6b08f495f8541f23abde995a5b9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adcd67c9-2bae-5a2f-88ce-cd3143fc6cad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/f4d4d52cb24bd1e3dd4bc37736533693ff89e6b08f495f8541f23abde995a5b9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286136} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f391e2-7e15-554e-8227-03ab04293d1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7364bd3c-e82a-5e8d-b8cd-06a6c72405af" :question/math-academy-id "q-81831" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/f7f75505591eb8bf407e63b4255ae65548bde65921f6f00cca9031191ddc0905.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/77936c4115b37d79ae301a93b218ecd23114ae4ad42d8298b8f058c1f50e82a2.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians **clockwise** about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ **clockwise** by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/ab6a3ec35954243105f9bb6c9f1f2cc21c02358f90855f9f36f0a590092eb93d.png)"}]}
